      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 European Society of Endocrinology.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "CONTEXT",
            "content": "The urea cycle is a rate-limiting step for amino acid nitrogen elimination. The rate of urea synthesis is a true indicator of whole-body protein catabolism. Testosterone reduces protein and nitrogen loss. The effect of testosterone on hepatic urea synthesis in humans has not been studied."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To determine whether testosterone reduces hepatic urea production."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "An open-label study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS AND INTERVENTION",
            "content": "Eight hypogonadal men were studied at baseline, and after two weeks of transdermal testosterone replacement (Testogel, 100\u2009mg/day)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MAIN OUTCOMES MEASURES",
            "content": "The rate of hepatic urea synthesis was measured by the urea turnover technique using stable isotope methodology, with 15N2-urea as tracer. Whole-body leucine turnover was measured, from which leucine rate of appearance (LRa), an index of protein breakdown and leucine oxidation (Lox), a measure of irreversible protein loss, were calculated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Testosterone administration significantly reduced the rate of hepatic urea production (from 544.4\u2009±\u200971.8 to 431.7\u2009±\u200968.3\u2009µmol/min; P\u2009<\u20090.01), which was paralleled by a significant reduction in serum urea concentration. Testosterone treatment significantly reduced net protein loss, as measured by percent Lox/LRa, by 19.3\u2009±\u20095.8% (P\u2009<\u20090.05). There was a positive association between Lox and hepatic urea production at baseline (r2\u2009=\u20090.60, P\u2009<\u20090.05) and after testosterone administration (r2\u2009=\u20090.59, P\u2009<\u20090.05)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Testosterone replacement reduces protein loss and hepatic urea synthesis. We conclude that testosterone regulates whole-body protein metabolism by suppressing the urea cycle."
          }
        ]
      },
